 <!-- FAQ -->
 <section id="faq" class="mb-16">
 <div class="bg-slate-50 rounded-2xl p-8 border border-slate-200 shadow-sm">
 <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-brandBlue uppercase italic mb-8 text-center">8. Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
 <div class="space-y-6 max-w-3xl mx-auto">
 <div class="bg-white rounded-xl p-6">
 <h3 class="font-black text-brandBlue mb-2">Wireless charging market size 2026, is there still room for new OEM brands?</h3>
 <p class="text-slate-600 text-sm faq-answer">The global wireless charging market reached $18.4 billion in 2026, growing at 24.2% CAGR toward $83.8 billion by 2033 (Coherent Market Insights/Fortune Business Insights). Qi2.2 (25W) is the fastest-growing segment at 38% CAGR. With 69.6% of new WPC certifications at 25W, the market is shifting upward but far from saturated, room remains for brands entering with differentiated Qi2.2 25W products, particularly in automotive (46.7% CAGR) and multi-device 3-in-1 stations.</p>
 </div>
 <div class="bg-white rounded-xl p-6">
 <h3 class="font-black text-brandBlue mb-2">Which phones support Qi2 15W+, should OEM brands stock both Qi2 and Qi2.2?</h3>
 <p class="text-slate-600 text-sm faq-answer">iPhone 16 and 17 series support Qi2 15W with iOS 26 enabling full Qi2.2 25W. Samsung Galaxy S26 supports Qi2-Ready (magnetic case required). Google Pixel 2026 supports Qi2 15W natively. For OEM inventory planning: stock Qi2 15W as the volume baseline (compatible with all Qi2 phones), add Qi2.2 25W as the premium tier for iPhone 17/Pro and flagship Android. Do not stock original Qi (5W, no magnets), it is obsolete for new OEM products in 2026.</p>
 </div>
 <div class="bg-white rounded-xl p-6">
 <h3 class="font-black text-brandBlue mb-2">Should OEM brands switch to Qi2.2 25W now or wait, what's the price difference vs Qi2 15W?</h3>
 <p class="text-slate-600 text-sm faq-answer">Qi2.2 doubles maximum wireless charging power from 15W to 25W while maintaining Qi2's Magnetic Power Profile (MPP) alignment. Hardware changes required: upgraded coil modules with tighter coupling tolerances, new thermal management ICs handling 67% more heat, and chipsets supporting the extended power profile. The BOM adder is $1.50-3.00/unit for Qi2.2 vs Qi2. OEM strategy: launch Qi2 for volume SKUs now, target Qi2.2 for premium SKUs shipping Q4 2026+. Qi2.2 certification velocity (637 products as of Feb 2026 with 69.62% at 25W) signals this will be the de facto standard within 12-18 months.</p>
 </div>
 <div class="bg-white rounded-xl p-6">
 <h3 class="font-black text-brandBlue mb-2">Are semi-solid-state battery power banks commercially available for OEM procurement?</h3>
 <p class="text-slate-600 text-sm faq-answer">Yes, semi-solid-state power banks entered mass production in 2026. BMX (SolidSafe Air) ships at 6.8mm thickness. ELECOM (DE-C85-5000) launched with a 2,000-cycle lifespan, 4× longer than traditional Li-polymer. These cells offer 30% higher energy density and near-zero thermal runaway risk compared to standard Li-polymer. OEM MOQ: 500-1,000 units, FOB 2-3× Li-polymer but commands 2-3× retail premium ($60-100+).</p>
 </div>
 <div class="bg-white rounded-xl p-6">
 <h3 class="font-black text-brandBlue mb-2">What five technology trends should B2B buyers prioritize for 2026-2027 OEM planning?</h3>
 <p class="text-slate-600 text-sm faq-answer">(1) GaN V is now the baseline above 45W, multi-port configurations with ≥92% efficiency are standard. (2) Qi2.2 25W replaces 15W as the default magnetic charging tier. (3) Semi-solid-state batteries enter commercial production with 2,000-cycle lifespan. (4) USB-C PD 3.2 mandates AVS + FRS for all new >27W certifications. (5) CCC traceability QR codes become mandatory for China-market products. OEM brands should audit current SKUs against these five baselines immediately.</p>
 </div>
 <div class="bg-white rounded-xl p-6">
 <h3 class="font-black text-brandBlue mb-2">What is China's new CCC traceability QR code and how does it affect OEM procurement?</h3>
 <p class="text-slate-600 text-sm faq-answer">Starting 2026, China's CCC (China Compulsory Certification) for power banks and chargers requires a unique traceable QR code on every product linking to manufacturing batch data, cell origin, and certification validity. For OEM buyers: non-CCC-certified power banks are prohibited from production, sale, and export from China. Verify your factory has CCC-certified product lines, non-compliant factories face shutdown. Amazon and EU customs increasingly check CCC QR validity for China-origin power banks. WOWOHCOOL products carry full CCC certification with traceable QR codes.</p>
 </div>
 <div class="bg-white rounded-xl p-6">
 <h3 class="font-black text-brandBlue mb-2">What should B2B buyers budget for new technology adoption in their 2026-2027 OEM product lines?</h3>
 <p class="text-slate-600 text-sm faq-answer">GaN V charger OEM: MOQ 500 units, FOB $5-8/unit at 65W. Qi2.2 wireless charger: WPC certification $3,000-8,000, 10-17 weeks timeline. Semi-solid-state power bank: MOQ 500-1,000, FOB $14-22/unit for 10,000mAh (2-3× Li-polymer but 2-3× retail premium). Total launch budget for a 3-SKU line (GaN charger + Qi2 pad + power bank): approximately $15,000-35,000 including tooling, certification, samples, and first production deposit.</p>
 </div>
 <div class="bg-white rounded-xl p-6">
 <h3 class="font-black text-brandBlue mb-2">How do OEM buyers plan their charging accessory product line for 2027?</h3>
 <p class="text-slate-600 text-sm faq-answer">Three-phase roadmap: Phase 1 (Q3 2026), audit your current SKUs against the 2026 baseline (GaN V above 45W, Qi2.2 25W, USB-C PD 3.2). Phase out silicon-only chargers and non-Qi2 wireless products immediately. Phase 2 (Q4 2026), launch GaN V multi-port chargers as volume drivers and Qi2.2 magnetic power banks as premium differentiators. Phase 3 (Q1-Q2 2027), add semi-solid-state power banks to the premium tier. <strong>WOWOHCOOL provides OEM/ODM across all five technology trends with MOQ from 500 units. <a href="/contact/" class="text-brandBlue hover:text-brandOrange underline">Request 2027 product line planning consultation.</a></strong></p>
 </div>
